00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:56.377
Okay.

00:00:56.377 --> 00:00:59.238
So why do we care about forms?

00:00:59.238 --> 00:01:03.405
Well, forms are the linchpin
to all online interactions.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:08.787
Forms are important for
registration, for revenue,

00:01:08.787 --> 00:01:13.748
to grow our community, and
participation in sharing,

00:01:13.748 --> 00:01:17.748
and any task that requires
entering information.

00:01:19.069 --> 00:01:19.919
So, just think.

00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:23.437
There'd be no cat videos
on YouTube without a form,

00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.253
or none of us would be
here at this conference

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:29.224
with registering for the
conference using a form.

00:01:29.224 --> 00:01:33.391
They're incredibly important
to our interactions with life.

00:01:34.303 --> 00:01:37.730
So, Luke Wroblewski, who's
the kind of gradnfather

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.381
of webform design, said
that "Webforms are often

00:01:41.381 --> 00:01:45.880
"the last and most important
mile in a long journey."

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.504
And this really resonated
with me 'cause I can tell you

00:01:48.504 --> 00:01:51.455
previously, I was working in
an agency where we were doing

00:01:51.455 --> 00:01:55.079
drupal websites, and as
the information architect

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:58.765
and UX designer, I was doing
a lot of things, and mostly

00:01:58.765 --> 00:02:01.580
kind of our forms went
something like this.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.231
About two to three days before
launch, somebody would say,

00:02:04.231 --> 00:02:05.940
"Julie, do we have a contact form?"

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.251
And I'd go, "Oh shit, I
forgot the contact form."

00:02:08.251 --> 00:02:12.009
So, I would end up then having
to enable the webform module

00:02:12.009 --> 00:02:14.399
or remind a developer to put
the webform module in the site

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:16.928
and just kind of throw it together.

00:02:16.928 --> 00:02:20.406
So, Luke's quote really rang
true and for me personally,

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.025
which is why I started
researching really how can I make

00:02:23.025 --> 00:02:26.195
the best and most effective form?

00:02:26.195 --> 00:02:30.547
So, how much do we think
a bad form can cost you?

00:02:30.547 --> 00:02:32.762
Well, I mean, the real
answer is priceless, right?

00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:35.053
But I can tell you that for Expedia,

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:37.136
it was about $12 million.

00:02:38.539 --> 00:02:41.556
They found that users
were going on the site,

00:02:41.556 --> 00:02:44.633
entering their travel
information, going through

00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:48.652
the whole process and
then clicking Buy Now,

00:02:48.652 --> 00:02:51.862
but not completing the transactions.

00:02:51.862 --> 00:02:54.634
This was happening
countless amount of times,

00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:56.996
so what's up with that?

00:02:56.996 --> 00:03:00.628
Well, it turns out that
they had a field under

00:03:00.628 --> 00:03:03.658
First Name, Last Name for Company,

00:03:03.658 --> 00:03:05.688
and users were entering
their, on the billing,

00:03:05.688 --> 00:03:08.104
this was on the billing
screen, so users were entering

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:09.834
their company--

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.584
I mean, entering their bank
account, like their bank name

00:03:12.584 --> 00:03:15.589
in the Company field, and then
entering their bank address

00:03:15.589 --> 00:03:18.580
in the Address field, so
credit card transactions

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.190
were failing because,
of course, the Address

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.684
was the bank address, and
not, like, their actual

00:03:23.684 --> 00:03:26.451
home address that the
credit card company knew.

00:03:26.451 --> 00:03:28.433
So after doing a little bit
of research, they realized

00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:31.077
if they just removed that Company field,

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.052
transactions increased
overnight, and over the course

00:03:34.052 --> 00:03:36.817
of that year, they earned
an additional $12 million

00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:37.734
in revenue.

00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:42.916
So, y'all, this is all
because of one form field.

00:03:44.068 --> 00:03:47.265
And the thing is is that UX
designers and professionals

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:50.196
in this industry, we know better.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:53.988
Studies have shown that forms
that follow basic usability

00:03:53.988 --> 00:03:57.423
guidelines are faster to
complete and almost twice

00:03:57.423 --> 00:04:00.840
as likely to be submitted without errors,

00:04:01.717 --> 00:04:05.644
so, and our challenge is
is that forms are annoying.

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:08.447
They're a thing in between
the person and what they're

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:09.467
looking for.

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.565
They're that thing you have to
go through to get something,

00:04:12.565 --> 00:04:13.814
right?

00:04:13.814 --> 00:04:16.931
So, it's kind of, it's a
blocker often to something else

00:04:16.931 --> 00:04:20.514
why someone would even
go through the form.

00:04:21.683 --> 00:04:25.944
And, forms really have
lasting consequences.

00:04:25.944 --> 00:04:28.967
I can tell you that my
friend Missy works for,

00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:30.211
in D.C. as someone,

00:04:30.211 --> 00:04:31.899
I was telling her I was coming
to the conference to talk

00:04:31.899 --> 00:04:34.523
about forms, and in my research,
I've been thinking more

00:04:34.523 --> 00:04:36.798
about forms, and she said,
"Let me tell you about a form."

00:04:36.798 --> 00:04:39.129
So, she works with folks
who are on disability

00:04:39.129 --> 00:04:41.977
and who are getting back to
work, so she was with a client

00:04:41.977 --> 00:04:45.191
one day and they were filling
out a form for the client

00:04:45.191 --> 00:04:48.594
to work at the local Harris
Teeter, which is a grocery store

00:04:48.594 --> 00:04:50.429
chain in the United States,

00:04:50.429 --> 00:04:54.989
and Harris Teeter had recently
redesigned their website,

00:04:54.989 --> 00:04:57.568
so they're filling out the
form, name, information,

00:04:57.568 --> 00:05:00.799
you know, your basic online application,

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:03.988
and in the State field,
they were in Virginia,

00:05:03.988 --> 00:05:07.962
she entered VA, which is the
state abbreviation for Virginia

00:05:07.962 --> 00:05:10.779
and they got an error
message, so they went back,

00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:11.618
and the client said,

00:05:11.618 --> 00:05:14.061
"Maybe we need to type
out V-I-R-G-I-N-I-A,"

00:05:14.061 --> 00:05:16.728
and still, got an error message,

00:05:17.594 --> 00:05:20.556
so they actually quit, they
left, and that client of hers

00:05:20.556 --> 00:05:23.022
left feeling like he was
not smart enough to work

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:26.681
at Harris Teeter, and that
is actually our fault,

00:05:26.681 --> 00:05:28.890
that's our fault as designers,
developers, that someone

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:32.300
who should have been QA-ing
that site and missed it,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.263
so it's just important to
remember that these decisions

00:05:34.263 --> 00:05:36.160
are things that we often
forget about really can

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.327
affect people.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.355
So here's an application
form for an online,

00:05:42.355 --> 00:05:44.732
for a job to work at Ladybird
and this is horrible.

00:05:44.732 --> 00:05:47.467
Imagine trying to go through this.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.048
Here's an online application.

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:50.965
Everything is required.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:53.242
It's painful.

00:05:54.637 --> 00:05:55.470
(chuckles)

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:56.303
Here's an online application.

00:05:56.303 --> 00:06:00.136
I've got a select list
that has every type of,

00:06:01.548 --> 00:06:03.736
like, every type of job but
it actually doesn't have

00:06:03.736 --> 00:06:07.653
my job in it, so I don't
know what to fill out.

00:06:09.225 --> 00:06:12.261
All right, so, now we're
going to talk about what makes

00:06:12.261 --> 00:06:14.399
an effective form.

00:06:14.399 --> 00:06:16.854
So you might say, well, Julie,
one that follows usability

00:06:16.854 --> 00:06:18.518
guidelines, like we talked about earlier,

00:06:18.518 --> 00:06:21.587
and I'd say you're 100%
correct, but for today,

00:06:21.587 --> 00:06:23.872
we're going to focus on
three guiding principles,

00:06:23.872 --> 00:06:26.692
which is to reduce cognitive load,

00:06:26.692 --> 00:06:30.442
to help prevent errors,
and to make it human.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:36.099
Okay, so, reducing cognitive
load means that we want to,

00:06:36.099 --> 00:06:39.299
to me, it means we want
the form to be scannable

00:06:39.299 --> 00:06:42.010
and have feelings of simplicity.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.468
So, first off, we're going
to talk about labels.

00:06:45.468 --> 00:06:49.635
A label is the thing that
is in red here, circled.

00:06:51.109 --> 00:06:55.276
Now, we know that top-line
labels are best for faster form

00:06:56.509 --> 00:06:58.324
completion times.

00:06:58.324 --> 00:07:01.149
This is because it's easier
to scan, and the reduced

00:07:01.149 --> 00:07:05.199
amount of eye movement needed
when going through the form,

00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:08.550
so it's always best to
have top-aligned labels.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.490
It's also important to
have your label close

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.520
to the input box because that
way the eye visually knows

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.075
they're tied together.

00:07:17.075 --> 00:07:19.801
I'd also like to recommend
not to use all caps

00:07:19.801 --> 00:07:23.224
because it can feel like
you're screaming at someone.

00:07:23.224 --> 00:07:24.915
And that's not cool.

00:07:24.915 --> 00:07:29.054
Additionally, it's really
important that we don't use

00:07:29.054 --> 00:07:30.221
inline labels.

00:07:31.168 --> 00:07:32.548
Does anyone know what an,

00:07:32.548 --> 00:07:34.628
does anyone want a reminder
about inline labels?

00:07:34.628 --> 00:07:37.839
Basically, inline labels are
when you have a form field

00:07:37.839 --> 00:07:41.390
and the label for that
form, like, user name

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.328
is in the form box itself.

00:07:44.328 --> 00:07:48.173
The problem with using inline
labels are that once someone

00:07:48.173 --> 00:07:51.182
clicks in the form, their
instructions go away,

00:07:51.182 --> 00:07:54.375
and like, we don't
remember things, as humans,

00:07:54.375 --> 00:07:56.568
we just don't, so, someone's got to out,

00:07:56.568 --> 00:07:57.779
like, click out again.

00:07:57.779 --> 00:07:59.393
It's making someone work harder.

00:07:59.393 --> 00:08:02.686
We're not helping them
reduce the cognitive load.

00:08:02.686 --> 00:08:06.558
Also, inline labels, someone
can think it's already

00:08:06.558 --> 00:08:09.774
filled out, so you just kind of skip it.

00:08:09.774 --> 00:08:13.514
And, according to an eye tracking
study from Norman Nielsen,

00:08:13.514 --> 00:08:14.513
blank, like,

00:08:14.513 --> 00:08:18.107
not inline labels, like
blank fields are actually

00:08:18.107 --> 00:08:20.663
more recognizable and draw the attention,

00:08:20.663 --> 00:08:24.667
eye's attention more
than inline labels, so,

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:27.417
remember our labels, top-aligned.

00:08:28.361 --> 00:08:32.284
Okay, we also, let's
use sequential buttons.

00:08:32.284 --> 00:08:36.336
What I mean by this is put the
primary action on the right

00:08:36.336 --> 00:08:38.453
and the secondary action on the left.

00:08:38.453 --> 00:08:41.098
So if you want someone to
submit, put it on the right.

00:08:41.098 --> 00:08:42.758
If you want them to go to
the next screen, put it

00:08:42.758 --> 00:08:44.061
on the right.

00:08:44.061 --> 00:08:47.687
Like, in this example,
we're going to delete.

00:08:47.687 --> 00:08:49.944
That's what someone's
intended to do, so it depends

00:08:49.944 --> 00:08:52.158
on your situation what
your primary action is.

00:08:52.158 --> 00:08:54.756
The reason why I say this
is because this follows

00:08:54.756 --> 00:08:59.647
most of our mental models
about which direction to go,

00:08:59.647 --> 00:09:01.960
so on the right is typically next,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.607
think about your web browser,
like the buttons at the top.

00:09:04.607 --> 00:09:08.803
Back is always left, so I
think this just helps us,

00:09:08.803 --> 00:09:11.903
helps the form be easier
to fill out if we know the,

00:09:11.903 --> 00:09:13.934
as designers, what we want people to do,

00:09:13.934 --> 00:09:16.211
put it on the right side.

00:09:16.211 --> 00:09:17.044
Cool?

00:09:18.192 --> 00:09:19.764
Oh yeah, don't use reset.

00:09:19.764 --> 00:09:22.822
That should never be a thing, ever.

00:09:22.822 --> 00:09:26.320
All right, also, let's
make our forms one column.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.069
This is for scannability and readability,

00:09:29.069 --> 00:09:30.089
and it's also,

00:09:30.089 --> 00:09:32.585
this includes radial
buttons and check boxes.

00:09:32.585 --> 00:09:35.338
Also, this is awesome
if you, of course, have

00:09:35.338 --> 00:09:40.092
a responsive presence as well,
because it's already aligned

00:09:40.092 --> 00:09:41.425
as it should be.

00:09:43.116 --> 00:09:46.126
All right, so now let's
talk about options.

00:09:46.126 --> 00:09:47.876
And select lists, drop downs.

00:09:47.876 --> 00:09:50.537
So, if you have, it's
recommended like, we,

00:09:50.537 --> 00:09:54.351
our brain can kind of absorb
about five things at one time

00:09:54.351 --> 00:09:56.761
so if you have a select list,

00:09:56.761 --> 00:09:58.489
like so you have five option,

00:09:58.489 --> 00:10:03.036
if you want to actually show
all those words on the page,

00:10:03.036 --> 00:10:06.592
if you have between six
and 26, you can put those

00:10:06.592 --> 00:10:09.512
in a select list, but
if it's more than 26,

00:10:09.512 --> 00:10:12.012
try and use contextual search.

00:10:14.372 --> 00:10:16.675
All right, another way we're
going to reduce cognitive load

00:10:16.675 --> 00:10:21.607
is to match our field length
to estimated text size,

00:10:21.607 --> 00:10:24.312
so, in this example, an expiry date,

00:10:24.312 --> 00:10:26.241
that's a, you know, like
month/month, year/year.

00:10:26.241 --> 00:10:27.447
That makes sense.

00:10:27.447 --> 00:10:31.682
Zip or postal code is a little
too big here in my opinion,

00:10:31.682 --> 00:10:33.217
but, and even though all your,

00:10:33.217 --> 00:10:35.754
I work with a visual
designer and he'll say,

00:10:35.754 --> 00:10:37.375
but Julia, it's not lined up properly,

00:10:37.375 --> 00:10:39.523
and I'll say, I don't care,
like, someone's going to think

00:10:39.523 --> 00:10:43.585
that they might be messing
something up, or it's like,

00:10:43.585 --> 00:10:46.234
if we know how long something
needs to be, that's as long

00:10:46.234 --> 00:10:49.984
as the text that, field
input size should be.

00:10:51.856 --> 00:10:52.689
Right?

00:10:53.618 --> 00:10:58.130
Okay, and let's group related
information in our forms.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.495
This is basically applying
content strategy to your form,

00:11:01.495 --> 00:11:03.596
so we know how to do this.

00:11:03.596 --> 00:11:07.356
Put things together that
make sense together.

00:11:07.356 --> 00:11:11.439
Yeah, once again, I know
you know how to do this.

00:11:12.962 --> 00:11:14.932
All right, use pre-fills when you can.

00:11:14.932 --> 00:11:19.099
Pre-fills are like hooking
into Google APIs to help

00:11:20.295 --> 00:11:23.898
bring in an address, or if
you're asking for someone's

00:11:23.898 --> 00:11:27.671
email when someone use the @
sign, then maybe prompting them

00:11:27.671 --> 00:11:31.897
up with @gmail.com or hotmail, et cetera.

00:11:31.897 --> 00:11:34.324
This is just another helpful
way to get someone through

00:11:34.324 --> 00:11:35.157
the form.

00:11:37.071 --> 00:11:40.328
Okay, also, we want to show progress.

00:11:40.328 --> 00:11:42.785
This is especially
helpful on longer forms.

00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:47.078
Give someone context for where
they are, in different steps.

00:11:47.078 --> 00:11:51.256
It gives them a sense of
place, and this is typically

00:11:51.256 --> 00:11:54.173
pretty good for onboarding as well.

00:11:56.544 --> 00:12:00.711
Okay, we also want to add
attributes for usability purposes

00:12:01.648 --> 00:12:06.484
so let's say if you are, an
attribute is a piece of code

00:12:06.484 --> 00:12:08.667
that you put in your form
or your developer puts in

00:12:08.667 --> 00:12:11.682
your form, that it's
basically saying, like,

00:12:11.682 --> 00:12:13.697
I know what you're going to
put in so I'm going to help

00:12:13.697 --> 00:12:16.503
you with that, so, for
instance, if you know it's going

00:12:16.503 --> 00:12:18.180
to be a telephone number,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.744
you an put in the attribute
tel, which, if you're on

00:12:21.744 --> 00:12:25.976
a mobile device, that's how
the number keypad pops up

00:12:25.976 --> 00:12:28.517
instead of, you know, the alphabet keypad,

00:12:28.517 --> 00:12:32.350
so that's just another
way we can help people.

00:12:34.976 --> 00:12:39.636
Okay, an input mask also
are important for reducing

00:12:39.636 --> 00:12:40.704
cognitive load.

00:12:40.704 --> 00:12:42.800
This is just helping people
format, so if you know

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.913
it's going to be a telephone
number or a credit card

00:12:44.913 --> 00:12:48.762
number, this is like
allowing that little dash

00:12:48.762 --> 00:12:51.403
and the parentheses to be
there so the user does not

00:12:51.403 --> 00:12:53.640
have to fill those out.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.571
And, doing them right is key.

00:12:55.571 --> 00:12:57.873
It's also important that
these aren't really going

00:12:57.873 --> 00:13:01.456
to show up unless you're
on focus, as well.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:05.643
All right, so that was,

00:13:05.643 --> 00:13:07.298
once again, I'm going through
a lot of things on how

00:13:07.298 --> 00:13:10.516
to go through a form, but that
was how we can help reduce

00:13:10.516 --> 00:13:12.224
cognitive load, but now
we're going to talk about

00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:14.697
how to help prevent errors.

00:13:14.697 --> 00:13:18.876
And this is the practice
of basically putting up a,

00:13:18.876 --> 00:13:22.045
putting up a fence, like
helping people not make errors,

00:13:22.045 --> 00:13:24.378
exactly what it sounds like.

00:13:26.032 --> 00:13:29.036
Okay, first off, field validation.

00:13:29.036 --> 00:13:31.987
So, we want to specify errors in line,

00:13:31.987 --> 00:13:34.891
and we want to validate items per field.

00:13:34.891 --> 00:13:37.201
This is an example from mint.com.

00:13:37.201 --> 00:13:38.696
I thought they did it really well.

00:13:38.696 --> 00:13:41.373
They had nice, clear context.

00:13:41.373 --> 00:13:43.369
They had nice examples for me.

00:13:43.369 --> 00:13:45.919
I mean, nice descriptions
for what I was doing wrong,

00:13:45.919 --> 00:13:49.174
for telling me, like, hey
this is what you can be doing.

00:13:49.174 --> 00:13:51.947
We all know how frustrating
is it, after you filled

00:13:51.947 --> 00:13:54.264
out a form and then pushed
Submit, and then get your

00:13:54.264 --> 00:13:55.697
sea of errors.

00:13:55.697 --> 00:13:59.744
This is slowing us down and
making someone not get to

00:13:59.744 --> 00:14:03.426
that thing they're hoping to
get to at the end of the form.

00:14:03.426 --> 00:14:07.188
Also, with this, it's
important that we don't show

00:14:07.188 --> 00:14:09.998
in line validation on focus.

00:14:09.998 --> 00:14:11.599
We want to show it on blur.

00:14:11.599 --> 00:14:14.976
That means don't wait 'til
someone clicks in the input,

00:14:14.976 --> 00:14:15.906
like, we don't want to,

00:14:15.906 --> 00:14:18.029
like, once you click in,
you want to wait 'til they

00:14:18.029 --> 00:14:20.700
click out to show the
error message, if there is

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.341
an error message because
if you do it on focus,

00:14:23.341 --> 00:14:24.865
which I see sometimes,
it's like you're basically

00:14:24.865 --> 00:14:29.525
yelling at someone before they've
even tried to fill it out.

00:14:29.525 --> 00:14:30.907
It's like saying, hey,
you've done this wrong,

00:14:30.907 --> 00:14:32.255
and you haven't even tried yet!

00:14:32.255 --> 00:14:36.422
It's just really a negative
experience (chuckles).

00:14:37.723 --> 00:14:41.890
Okay, so we also, we want
to leave select lists blank,

00:14:42.949 --> 00:14:45.032
so, this is because it's,

00:14:47.015 --> 00:14:49.178
select lists are really
easy to skip and if you're

00:14:49.178 --> 00:14:50.350
scanning a form,

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:53.124
if people are scanning a form
and it's already filled out

00:14:53.124 --> 00:14:54.665
or let's say there's the default,

00:14:54.665 --> 00:14:57.687
if it's an alpha-order
list and the default is A,

00:14:57.687 --> 00:14:59.036
whatever that is for you,

00:14:59.036 --> 00:15:02.356
chances are that someone's
just going to skip that

00:15:02.356 --> 00:15:04.833
and not change it, so this is helping,

00:15:04.833 --> 00:15:08.489
this is making someone basically,
potentially give an error

00:15:08.489 --> 00:15:09.656
or a input at,

00:15:10.892 --> 00:15:14.886
an answer they might
not have meant to give.

00:15:14.886 --> 00:15:18.029
So, static values are
really easy to skip and when

00:15:18.029 --> 00:15:20.605
it's blank, similar to what
we talked about earlier,

00:15:20.605 --> 00:15:25.132
when it's blank, it's actually
more obvious to someone

00:15:25.132 --> 00:15:28.011
that they need to fill it out.

00:15:28.011 --> 00:15:32.178
However, if you know that 90%
of your users are going to

00:15:33.271 --> 00:15:37.609
choose one option, you can use
a smart default in that case

00:15:37.609 --> 00:15:40.224
so for instance, here, they,

00:15:40.224 --> 00:15:42.918
I think they were using
geolocation to know that I was

00:15:42.918 --> 00:15:45.680
in the United States, so
they were able to say,

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:50.041
hey, okay, we're going to
pre-populate United States for you

00:15:50.041 --> 00:15:53.065
but in general, it's always
better to leave select lists

00:15:53.065 --> 00:15:56.565
blank if you don't have that kind of data.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.889
Okay, so help text.

00:16:00.889 --> 00:16:04.200
Sometimes you need additional,
like, hey, here's what we

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.624
mean by this, and when
you're in your form,

00:16:06.624 --> 00:16:07.638
which is completely fine.

00:16:07.638 --> 00:16:11.805
It's recommended to put help
text underneath the field box.

00:16:14.733 --> 00:16:17.171
Luckily for anyone who
works with drupal sites,

00:16:17.171 --> 00:16:19.579
this is how drupal does
it natively, so, it helps,

00:16:19.579 --> 00:16:20.412
it's nice.

00:16:23.366 --> 00:16:27.396
Okay, so it's also nice to
differentiate button styles.

00:16:27.396 --> 00:16:31.563
Kind of similarly to making
them sequential, the primary,

00:16:32.816 --> 00:16:35.000
let's make it more visually obvious.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:37.105
Let's say, okay, this
is big, this is bright,

00:16:37.105 --> 00:16:39.494
this is what we want you
to be doing or what we

00:16:39.494 --> 00:16:41.797
anticipate you doing.

00:16:41.797 --> 00:16:44.081
Once again, I think one of the
most frustrating experiences

00:16:44.081 --> 00:16:48.291
I personally have had is
to fill out a form and then

00:16:48.291 --> 00:16:51.791
accidentally push the wrong button, right?

00:16:54.074 --> 00:16:56.718
Okay, so now we're going
to talk about how to make

00:16:56.718 --> 00:16:57.718
forms human.

00:16:59.208 --> 00:17:01.491
Because forms really are kind of the,

00:17:01.491 --> 00:17:04.021
it can be, it's like
conversation almost that you're

00:17:04.021 --> 00:17:08.084
having with someone, and it's
a really impersonal thing,

00:17:08.084 --> 00:17:09.736
like, there's absolutely no,

00:17:09.736 --> 00:17:14.396
well, often, there's no
customization to it and there's,

00:17:14.396 --> 00:17:15.484
like, it can be really,

00:17:15.484 --> 00:17:17.493
they can be really dry, but
let's remember that there's

00:17:17.493 --> 00:17:19.736
actually a person on the
other side of this form

00:17:19.736 --> 00:17:21.838
who's filling it out.

00:17:21.838 --> 00:17:24.309
So as designers, it's important
that we make our forms

00:17:24.309 --> 00:17:27.085
as human as we possibly can,
so now we're going to talk

00:17:27.085 --> 00:17:29.918
about some tips on how to do that.

00:17:30.978 --> 00:17:34.076
Okay, similar to what Kyle was
talking about this morning,

00:17:34.076 --> 00:17:37.580
we want clear and simple
language and call to actions,

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:39.657
so it's important to
do your user research,

00:17:39.657 --> 00:17:42.654
know who your users are and
speak to them in a language

00:17:42.654 --> 00:17:45.128
that they understand.

00:17:45.128 --> 00:17:49.295
Labels and buttons should clearly
explain what they're doing

00:17:51.176 --> 00:17:55.939
and be written for the
user, so in this example,

00:17:55.939 --> 00:17:59.130
this is, I was looking at
a snowboard on Burton.com,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.233
and I said, oh sure, I'll
sign up for your newsletter.

00:18:01.233 --> 00:18:03.836
I really liked how they
said, I'm interested in gear

00:18:03.836 --> 00:18:06.880
for men, women, or kids, in the form.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.075
That was a really friendly
way to say, hey, how,

00:18:10.075 --> 00:18:14.242
in the future, how should
we market to this person?

00:18:17.659 --> 00:18:20.138
Also, we want to limit typing.

00:18:20.138 --> 00:18:23.643
Don't make people work any
harder than they need to.

00:18:23.643 --> 00:18:25.596
We wouldn't do that in real life.

00:18:25.596 --> 00:18:28.929
We don't want to do it on a form either.

00:18:31.567 --> 00:18:34.715
Okay, and we want to make it accessible.

00:18:34.715 --> 00:18:37.916
Web accessibility refers
to the inclusive practice

00:18:37.916 --> 00:18:41.240
of removing barriers that
prevent interaction with,

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:44.959
or access to websites by
people with disabilities.

00:18:44.959 --> 00:18:48.959
And, if a website or app
is designed, developed,

00:18:50.759 --> 00:18:54.205
and edited with accessibility
in mind, then all users

00:18:54.205 --> 00:18:58.372
have access to the same
information and functionality.

00:18:59.269 --> 00:19:03.604
And, sites and apps that
are designed accessible

00:19:03.604 --> 00:19:04.911
are actually better for everyone.

00:19:04.911 --> 00:19:06.961
Kind of, accessibility
practice is one of those things

00:19:06.961 --> 00:19:09.130
that once you do it, you
find a lot of hidden benefits

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:10.186
as well.

00:19:10.186 --> 00:19:13.095
For instance, ramps aren't
just only used by people

00:19:13.095 --> 00:19:14.011
in wheelchairs.

00:19:14.011 --> 00:19:18.094
Strollers and people with
bad knees, on crutches,

00:19:19.423 --> 00:19:21.168
or just someone who prefers
to take a ramp and not

00:19:21.168 --> 00:19:23.010
the stairs, right?

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:25.300
So, there's a lot of hidden
benefits in accessibility

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:27.687
and it's incredibly
important with forms as well.

00:19:27.687 --> 00:19:29.672
There's a lot written
about how to do this,

00:19:29.672 --> 00:19:33.839
on W3C, and really, if you
just code a form in a clean way

00:19:35.225 --> 00:19:37.058
it will be accessible.

00:19:38.084 --> 00:19:40.528
It's better for everyone.

00:19:40.528 --> 00:19:43.589
Okay, and we want to
value our users' time.

00:19:43.589 --> 00:19:47.075
Amanda mentioned earlier,
like, if it's only $10,

00:19:47.075 --> 00:19:47.942
she's not going to do it.

00:19:47.942 --> 00:19:49.994
We want to make sure we
are valuing someone's time

00:19:49.994 --> 00:19:52.163
as they're inputting,
they're there on the form,

00:19:52.163 --> 00:19:55.294
they're taking their time
out, we want to really think

00:19:55.294 --> 00:19:59.747
about the questions we're
asking, so in this example,

00:19:59.747 --> 00:20:02.891
I was redesigning the giving
form for Duke University

00:20:02.891 --> 00:20:06.196
and this is the old form, and
I said, it was really long

00:20:06.196 --> 00:20:08.666
and lot of stuff, like this
actually only the second half

00:20:08.666 --> 00:20:10.308
of the form in the screenshot.

00:20:10.308 --> 00:20:12.262
I couldn't fit the whole
thing, where you'd all be able

00:20:12.262 --> 00:20:13.644
to read it.

00:20:13.644 --> 00:20:17.268
In this box, Enter any
comments you have concerning

00:20:17.268 --> 00:20:21.487
your gift designations,
maximum 1,000 characters.

00:20:21.487 --> 00:20:24.165
Nobody was doing anything
with this text box,

00:20:24.165 --> 00:20:28.232
so people were often, people
were just entering things like

00:20:28.232 --> 00:20:31.215
"Say hi to Bob for me," or "Go Duke!"

00:20:31.215 --> 00:20:33.512
And it wasn't that,

00:20:33.512 --> 00:20:36.108
that data wasn't actually going anywhere,

00:20:36.108 --> 00:20:38.458
so we removed that.

00:20:38.458 --> 00:20:43.372
Also, with our user research,
we learned, with alumni dues,

00:20:43.372 --> 00:20:45.462
we could remove that from the redesign,

00:20:45.462 --> 00:20:46.938
we could remove that
from the new iteration

00:20:46.938 --> 00:20:50.406
because it wasn't just alumni
who were coming to the form,

00:20:50.406 --> 00:20:52.337
it was people who were also
giving because they really

00:20:52.337 --> 00:20:54.471
liked the basketball team.

00:20:54.471 --> 00:20:56.557
So, it's just really important
to make sure we think about

00:20:56.557 --> 00:21:00.388
who is going to be on this
form and do we really need

00:21:00.388 --> 00:21:03.493
to ask that, because I want to
make sure I value their time,

00:21:03.493 --> 00:21:06.076
just as you would in real life.

00:21:08.153 --> 00:21:10.650
Okay, and also consider the context.

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.339
People are going to be
filling out forms in all sorts

00:21:13.339 --> 00:21:15.897
of different places so we
want to think about where

00:21:15.897 --> 00:21:19.454
is someone going to most likely be?

00:21:19.454 --> 00:21:22.792
Social situations matter and
it includes forms as well

00:21:22.792 --> 00:21:26.625
so if you are, let's say
a car insurance form,

00:21:28.277 --> 00:21:30.764
you want to make a claim
on your car insurance,

00:21:30.764 --> 00:21:34.142
there's a possibility that
someone's filling out that form

00:21:34.142 --> 00:21:36.465
right after they've had
their accident, on the side

00:21:36.465 --> 00:21:38.341
of the road, so you want to
make sure you think about

00:21:38.341 --> 00:21:42.527
color contrast and, is
the text big enough?

00:21:42.527 --> 00:21:45.007
And are you using language
that empathizes with them?

00:21:45.007 --> 00:21:48.719
It might be a really scary
moment for that person, right?

00:21:48.719 --> 00:21:50.764
Or, if you're sick, if it's
a form to maybe request

00:21:50.764 --> 00:21:54.140
a visit at a doctor's office,
that person could be sick

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:56.009
when they're filling out the
form, so you want to make it

00:21:56.009 --> 00:21:59.836
really clear about what
to do and make, you know,

00:21:59.836 --> 00:22:04.003
make it as easy as possible
because situations matter.

00:22:06.773 --> 00:22:09.570
Okay, and, once again,
clear call to actions.

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.641
Right here, I'm really
talking about buttons because

00:22:12.641 --> 00:22:15.524
it's important to remind
people why they're filling out

00:22:15.524 --> 00:22:16.646
the form.

00:22:16.646 --> 00:22:19.310
This is an airline in
the U.S. called Delta,

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:22.835
and the button says Find
Flights, which I just thought

00:22:22.835 --> 00:22:25.699
was really clear, and I liked
that it was reminding me

00:22:25.699 --> 00:22:28.095
why I was filling out this form.

00:22:28.095 --> 00:22:30.845
Not just Submit or Go, let's say.

00:22:32.007 --> 00:22:34.784
This is also good for
accessibility, like if you have

00:22:34.784 --> 00:22:37.434
a home page and you have
a couple news snippets

00:22:37.434 --> 00:22:40.585
and then you want to say, like, more news,

00:22:40.585 --> 00:22:42.548
you want to show more news
and go to your news page,

00:22:42.548 --> 00:22:45.404
it's awesome to say More
News, not just More.

00:22:45.404 --> 00:22:48.654
So, we want to be as clear as possible.

00:22:50.733 --> 00:22:52.388
All right, so now I'm going
to go through some kind of

00:22:52.388 --> 00:22:55.065
common questions that people ask me about,

00:22:55.065 --> 00:22:56.594
because once I started talking about forms

00:22:56.594 --> 00:22:58.617
and thinking about forms,
it kind of comes to me,

00:22:58.617 --> 00:23:01.313
so I encourage you all to
do that too afterwards.

00:23:01.313 --> 00:23:04.298
Somebody would say, Julie,
forms, the shorter the better,

00:23:04.298 --> 00:23:05.131
right?

00:23:05.131 --> 00:23:07.175
And I'd say, totally depends.

00:23:07.175 --> 00:23:10.764
So much depends on the
context and it doesn't,

00:23:10.764 --> 00:23:15.239
like studies have shown
that long forms are fine,

00:23:15.239 --> 00:23:19.158
as long as the questions
are contextual and engaging,

00:23:19.158 --> 00:23:23.158
so really, it always does
come back to research.

00:23:24.759 --> 00:23:27.583
Or, optional versus required.

00:23:27.583 --> 00:23:29.682
Like, oh, hey, should we,

00:23:29.682 --> 00:23:33.110
should this field be required or optional?

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:34.590
Or, what's the deal with that?

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:38.462
It's best to mark your optional fields,

00:23:38.462 --> 00:23:42.026
not your required fields,
and I recommend marking

00:23:42.026 --> 00:23:46.115
your optional fields in
parentheses with the words optional

00:23:46.115 --> 00:23:47.543
in there.

00:23:47.543 --> 00:23:52.223
This is because not everyone
knows what that asterisk means.

00:23:52.223 --> 00:23:53.291
Right?

00:23:53.291 --> 00:23:58.037
And, the required is, like,
saying something is required

00:23:58.037 --> 00:23:58.892
is a little bit,

00:23:58.892 --> 00:24:01.366
is much more harsher than saying optional.

00:24:01.366 --> 00:24:04.706
Noting optional is more
friendly, and we know

00:24:04.706 --> 00:24:06.961
someone's there to fill
it out, so it's really not

00:24:06.961 --> 00:24:08.705
necessary to over,

00:24:08.705 --> 00:24:12.034
to show the required because
they know they have to fill out

00:24:12.034 --> 00:24:14.500
the fields to get to the next step, right?

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.167
So, marking optional is showing,

00:24:18.145 --> 00:24:22.697
it's showing that the
fields that aren't optional

00:24:22.697 --> 00:24:23.780
are required.

00:24:24.774 --> 00:24:26.476
Also, let's think about it.

00:24:26.476 --> 00:24:29.614
Required is really a developer,

00:24:29.614 --> 00:24:32.395
required is something that actually

00:24:32.395 --> 00:24:33.680
goes in the code to make sure

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:36.321
someone fills out the form,
so this is a design pattern

00:24:36.321 --> 00:24:39.935
that is being influenced
by code for us, right now,

00:24:39.935 --> 00:24:41.662
and we don't need to do that.

00:24:41.662 --> 00:24:44.995
It's totally fine to just mark optional.

00:24:46.704 --> 00:24:49.434
All right, so now we've got
some interactive time here.

00:24:49.434 --> 00:24:52.684
So, help me chat about what went wrong.

00:24:54.627 --> 00:24:58.699
Okay, so this one,
we've got inline labels,

00:24:58.699 --> 00:25:01.080
and I can tell you, I typed
in Durham, North Carolina

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:04.568
for my location and it
says location not found.

00:25:04.568 --> 00:25:06.901
I promise it's a real place.

00:25:09.105 --> 00:25:13.188
All right, here, my buttons
are all out of order.

00:25:14.168 --> 00:25:15.607
Let's see, it's not sequential.

00:25:15.607 --> 00:25:18.264
I'm getting an error
message before I submitted,

00:25:18.264 --> 00:25:20.243
and just like, how are
you going to have a form

00:25:20.243 --> 00:25:21.121
within a form?

00:25:21.121 --> 00:25:22.278
It's horribly unorganized.

00:25:22.278 --> 00:25:25.528
I don't have my content group together.

00:25:27.072 --> 00:25:29.469
Okay, here, I don't
know how to close this.

00:25:29.469 --> 00:25:31.024
I don't see a close button.

00:25:31.024 --> 00:25:33.024
This is not human.

00:25:33.024 --> 00:25:36.209
You wouldn't trap someone
in real life, would you?

00:25:36.209 --> 00:25:38.221
(audience chuckles)

00:25:38.221 --> 00:25:40.676
Oh, wow, I've got way
too many select lists.

00:25:40.676 --> 00:25:42.192
Let's see, they're not blank.

00:25:42.192 --> 00:25:46.497
We should really have Please
select not everywhere,

00:25:46.497 --> 00:25:50.644
and the input field, the
fields don't match the size

00:25:50.644 --> 00:25:52.792
of what's in there at all.

00:25:52.792 --> 00:25:54.910
Also, this is making me do math.

00:25:54.910 --> 00:25:56.397
If y'all can't read it out there, it says,

00:25:56.397 --> 00:26:00.545
Skill test question: 4
plus 10, divided by 5.

00:26:00.545 --> 00:26:01.503
What?

00:26:01.503 --> 00:26:02.670
This is crazy!

00:26:03.775 --> 00:26:05.858
These are real forms too.

00:26:06.877 --> 00:26:08.755
This one, I'm not even sure
what all went wrong here,

00:26:08.755 --> 00:26:13.039
but (chuckles), it's just bad.

00:26:13.039 --> 00:26:16.376
All right, so here we have
an example of in line errors

00:26:16.376 --> 00:26:20.403
that were given on focus, so, basically,

00:26:20.403 --> 00:26:23.539
I just clicked in First name,
and then I got this prompt,

00:26:23.539 --> 00:26:24.695
What's your first name?

00:26:24.695 --> 00:26:28.427
But already got error messages
in all the other fields.

00:26:28.427 --> 00:26:29.673
I hadn't even filled it out yet.

00:26:29.673 --> 00:26:31.805
I hadn't even tried and it
was telling me I did it wrong

00:26:31.805 --> 00:26:33.648
already, right?

00:26:33.648 --> 00:26:34.481
It's sad.

00:26:36.018 --> 00:26:37.711
Okay, so while I just have
a couple minutes left,

00:26:37.711 --> 00:26:39.950
I'm going to do a quick chat
about how I kind of feel like

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:43.159
the future of form design's going.

00:26:43.159 --> 00:26:45.631
And I just wanted to put
that cat meme in here.

00:26:45.631 --> 00:26:49.162
Okay, so material design is,
as we all know, it's a style,

00:26:49.162 --> 00:26:51.906
or as you might know, it's a
style guide or a design trend

00:26:51.906 --> 00:26:52.811
from Google.

00:26:52.811 --> 00:26:57.372
It kind of breaks my top-align
rule that I chatted about

00:26:57.372 --> 00:26:59.387
in the beginning, but it
doesn't really break it because

00:26:59.387 --> 00:27:01.210
it's an inline label
and when you click in,

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:02.760
the label goes above.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.009
It's really cool.

00:27:04.009 --> 00:27:05.782
I think it works great.

00:27:05.782 --> 00:27:08.045
It's important, though,
to test with your users,

00:27:08.045 --> 00:27:09.743
your user base, to make
sure it works for them

00:27:09.743 --> 00:27:13.809
and also watch your
accessibility contrast ratios

00:27:13.809 --> 00:27:16.917
'cause often, I see a
light gray text on a white,

00:27:16.917 --> 00:27:18.667
with material design.

00:27:20.224 --> 00:27:22.801
Okay, conversational user interfaces.

00:27:22.801 --> 00:27:24.757
These are the chat box.

00:27:24.757 --> 00:27:27.180
Like, this is really
just a form in itself.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:29.704
It's a way that the user or
the person, the user, the human

00:27:29.704 --> 00:27:33.459
is giving interaction to
the company, to the website.

00:27:33.459 --> 00:27:36.199
It's successful in collecting data.

00:27:36.199 --> 00:27:38.928
But, I mean, it's kind of like,

00:27:38.928 --> 00:27:43.095
it's somewhere in the cousin
of form design, I'd say.

00:27:44.256 --> 00:27:47.508
Also, voice is a really cool
way that we're going to be

00:27:47.508 --> 00:27:49.258
interacting with our,

00:27:50.105 --> 00:27:52.906
with sites, brands and apps in the future,

00:27:52.906 --> 00:27:56.458
to get that same kind of
information that one might be

00:27:56.458 --> 00:27:59.077
filling out in a form, and I
can't wait to listen to Mark

00:27:59.077 --> 00:28:01.694
later today talk about voice.

00:28:01.694 --> 00:28:04.330
You know, I think in my
experience, usability heuristics

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:07.761
still hold true when we're
talking about voice UX,

00:28:07.761 --> 00:28:11.928
but, basically, I can't
wait to see where this goes.

00:28:13.049 --> 00:28:15.232
All right, so if you
forget everything I say,

00:28:15.232 --> 00:28:17.601
which is totally cool,
please remember, just these

00:28:17.601 --> 00:28:20.019
guiding principles, which
when you're talking about form

00:28:20.019 --> 00:28:22.367
design and you're
thinking about your forms,

00:28:22.367 --> 00:28:25.520
we want to help reduce cognitive load,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:29.138
help prevent errors, and make it human,

00:28:29.138 --> 00:28:33.364
and when in doubt, be sure
to talk to your users.

00:28:33.364 --> 00:28:34.702
So, thank you very much.

00:28:34.702 --> 00:28:36.301
I appreciate you all being here.

00:28:36.301 --> 00:28:39.301
(audience applauds)

